"Lightweight unsupervised image enhancement based on bilateral grid (https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S2667379726000975)\nciteturn9search0 [wordlim: 200] Published: 2 months ago; Crawled: 2 months ago; Lightweight unsupervised image enhancement based on bilateral grid ... This paper introduces a lightweight image enhancement network named Cycle Bilateral Generative Adversarial Networks (CBGAN), specifically designed for real-time visual perception in robotic systems.\n\n## Biomimetic Intelligence and Robotics\n\nDate: Available online 31 August 2026\n\nArticle: 100369\n\nIn Press, Journal Pre-proof\n\nResearch article\n\nOpen access\n\nUnder a Creative Commons license\n\nResearch Article\n\nLightweight unsupervised image enhancement based on bilateral grid\n\nAuthor links open overlay panel Chaoyi Liu, Zhulin An, Chuanguang Yang, Libo Huang, Yongjun Xu\n\nView PDF\n\nCite\n\nAdd to Mendeley\n\nShare\n\n10.1016/j.birob.2026.100369\n\n  * Article\n\n## Abstract\n\nVisual perception serves as the primary sensory modality for autonomous robots navigating and interacting with complex environments. However, robotic vision systems frequently encounter challenging illumination conditions—such as low-light indoor corridors, high-contrast outdoor scenes, and backlit industrial workspaces—that severely degrade the quality of captured images, directly compromising downstream robotic tasks including object detection, semantic segmentation, and visual SLAM. Deep learning-based methods have achieved remarkable success in image enhancement, but traditional approaches often necessitate paired datasets for training, incurring high costs for acquiring paired pre- and post-optimized images in real-world robotic scenarios. This paper introduces a lightweight image enhancement network named Cycle Bilateral Generative Adversarial Networks (CBGAN), specifically designed for real-time visual perception in robotic systems. Based on an unsupervised cyclic generative adversarial network architecture, CBGAN optimizes images by learning to predict both local and global affine transformations within the bilateral space in a low-resolution stream, and subsequently applying these transformations to the full-resolution domain. Specifically, CBGAN employs adversarial training, incorporating inverse mapping and cycle consistency loss, to ensure high-quality enhancement within an unsupervised training paradigm. In unsupervised scenarios, CBGAN outperforms existing state-of-the-art methods. Our network processes a 4 K image in tens of milliseconds. Compared with the conventional CycleGAN employing ResNet generators, our method achieves an approximate 95.8% reduction in parameter count and operates over 170 times faster, demonstrating immense potential for real-time deployment on resource-constrained robotic platforms.\n\n## Keywords\n\nImage enhancement\n\n;\n\nUnsupervised\n\n;\n\nLightweight--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\ndblp: Zero-Shot Illumination and Noise Estimation for Edge-Deployable Low-Light Image Enhancement in IoT Systems. (https://dblp.uni-trier.de/rec/journals/iotj/XiongMYLZJWW26.html)\nciteturn9search1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2 months ago; Zero-Shot Illumination and Noise Estimation for Edge-Deployable Low-Light Image Enhancement in IoT Systems.\n\n# \"Zero-Shot Illumination and Noise Estimation for Edge-Deployable Low-Light ...\"\n\n\nZhongxia Xiong et al. (2026)\n\n  *   * Image: use mirror server\n\nTrier\n\n  * Dagstuhl\n\n\n## Details and statistics\n\n  * show external API response\n\n    * JSON @ openalex.org\n\nsee also:\n\n    * API doc @ openalex.org\n\nDOI: 10.1109/JIOT.2026.3707835\n\naccess: closed\n\ntype: Journal Article\n\nmetadata version: 2026-08-31\n\n  * Image: Journal Articles\n\n    * view\n\n      * electronic edition via DOI\n      * unpaywalled version\n\n\n    * export record\n\n      * BibTeX\n      * RIS\n      * RDF N-Triples\n      * RDF Turtle\n      * RDF/XML\n      * XML\n      * plain text\n\ndblp key:\n\n      * journals/iotj/XiongMYLZJWW26\n\n      * https://dblp.org/rec/journals/iotj/XiongMYLZJWW26\n\nZhongxia XiongImage: 0000-0002-1694-6218, Jie MeiImage: 0009-0008-2179-4424, Ziying YaoImage: 0000-0001-5697-4275, Xuan LiuImage: 0009-0006-2635-911X, Baizhi Zhang, Yongzhi JiangImage: 0009-0009-5174-8311, Pengcheng WangImage: 0000-0002-2103-0554, Xinkai WuImage: 0000-0003-4238-0243:\nZero-Shot Illumination and Noise Estimation for Edge-Deployable Low-Light Image Enhancement in IoT Systems. IEEE Internet Things J. 13(17): 39626-39640 (2026)\n\nlast updated on 2026-08-31 15:37 CEST by the dblp team\n\n all metadata released as open data under CC0 1.0 license--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nReal-time edge-aware image processing with the bilateral grid (https://doi.org/10.1145/1276377.1276506)\nciteturn9search2 [wordlim: 200] Published: 19.3 years ago; Crawled: last month;   * Ji J Ma K Zhong B Zhou F Wu Q(2026)Deep bilateral learning for image interpolation Knowledge-Based Systems 10.1016/j.knosys.2026.116266 347:C Online publication date: 6-Aug-2026 ...   * Liu C An Z Yang C Huang L Xu Y(2026)Lightweight unsupervised image enhancement based on bilateral grid Biomimetic Intelligence and Robotics 10.1016/j.birob.2026.100369(100369)Online publication date: Aug-2026\n\narticle\n\nFree access\n\n# Real-time edge-aware image processing with the bilateral grid\n\nAuthors: Jiawen Chen, Sylvain Paris, Frédo DurandAuthors Info & Claims\n\nACM Transactions on Graphics (TOG), Volume 26, Issue 3\n\nPages 103 - es\n\nhttps://doi.org/10.1145/1276377.1276506\n\nPublished: 29 July 2007 Publication History\n\n## Abstract\n\nWe present a new data structure---the bilateral grid, that enables fast edge-aware image processing. By working in the bilateral grid, algorithms such as bilateral filtering, edge-aware painting, and local histogram equalization become simple manipulations that are both local and independent. We parallelize our algorithms on modern GPUs to achieve real-time frame rates on high-definition video. We demonstrate our method on a variety of applications such as image editing, transfer of photographic look, and contrast enhancement of medical images.\n\n## Supplementary Material\n\nMP4 File (pps102.mp4)\n\n[Input] iframe\n\nhttps://dl.acm.org/doi/10.1016/j.knosys.2026.116266\n\n  * Liu C An Z Yang C Huang L Xu Y(2026)Lightweight unsupervised image enhancement based on bilateral grid Biomimetic Intelligence and Robotics 10.1016/j.birob.2026.100369(100369)Online publication date: Aug-2026\n\nhttps://doi.org/10.1016/j.birob.2026.100369\n\n  * Zheng R Huang S Zhu L Zheng W Zhang Q(2026)Generative Texture Filtering Proceedings of the Special Interest Group on Computer Graphics and Interactive Techniques Conference Conference Papers 10.1145/3799902.3811147(1-11)Online publication date: 19-Jul-2026\n\nhttps://dl.acm.org/doi/10.1145/3799902.3811147\n\n  * Rosenthal A Berman D Hoshen Y Shamir A(2026)Progressive Photorealistic Simplification Proceedings of the Special Interest Group on Computer Graphics and Interactive Techniques Conference Conference Papers 10.1145/3799902.3811083(1-10)Online publication date: 19-Jul-2026\n\nhttps://dl.acm.org/doi/10.1145/3799902.3811083\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nFastSHADE: Fast Self-augmented Hierarchical Asymmetric Denoising for Efficient inference on mobile devices (https://www.catalyzex.com/paper/fastshade-fast-self-augmented-hierarchical)\nciteturn9search3 [wordlim: 200] Published: 7 months ago; Crawled: last month; # Title:FastSHADE: Fast Self-augmented Hierarchical Asymmetric Denoising for Efficient inference on mobile devices\n\n### Paper and Code\n\n# Title:FastSHADE: Fast Self-augmented Hierarchical Asymmetric Denoising for Efficient inference on mobile devices\n\n### Paper and Code\n\nRequest Code\n\nImage: Bookmark button\n\nImage: Alert button\n\nApr 11, 2026\n\nAuthors:Nikolay Falaleev\n\nFigures and Tables:\n\n[Input: Ask the author(s) a question! :)]\n\nAsk\n\nAbstract:Real-time image denoising is essential for modern mobile photography but remains challenging due to the strict latency and power constraints of edge devices. This paper presents FastSHADE (Fast Self-augmented Hierarchical Asymmetric Denoising), a lightweight U-Net-style network tailored for real-time, high-fidelity restoration on mobile GPUs. Our method features a multi-stage architecture incorporating a novel Asymmetric Frequency Denoising Block (AFDB) that decouples spatial structure extraction from high-frequency noise suppression to maximize efficiency, and a Spatially Gated Upsampler (SGU) that optimizes high-resolution skip connection fusion. To address generalization, we introduce an efficient Noise Shifting Self-Augmentation strategy that enhances data diversity without inducing domain shifts. Evaluations on the MAI2021 benchmark demonstrate that our scalable model family establishes a highly efficient speed-fidelity trade-off. Our base FastSHADE-M variant maintains real-time latency (<50 ms on a modern mobile GPU) while preserving structural integrity, and our scaled-up FastSHADE-XL establishes a new state-of-the-art for overall image quality. Ultimately, FastSHADE successfully bridges the gap between theoretical network efficiency and practical deployment for real-world mobile ISP pipelines.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nEnergy-Aware Deployment of Quantized Shallow-Water Image Enhancement on an Edge NPU for Underwater IoT Imaging (https://eurekamag.com/research/111/552/111552993.php)\nciteturn9search4 [wordlim: 200] Published: 10 months ago; Crawled: last month; # Energy-Aware Deployment of Quantized Shallow-Water Image Enhancement on an Edge NPU for Underwater IoT Imaging ... Underwater image enhancement (UIE) research is trending toward heavy models, while lightweight alternatives are judged by parameters and FLOPs: proxies that say little about latency and energy on the edge accelerators underwater IoT platforms deploy. ... 2021: A Method of Image Enhancement in Shallow Water with Underwater System Proceedings - 2021 International Symposium on Computer Technology and Information Science, ISCTIS 2021: 308-312\n\n# Energy-Aware Deployment of Quantized Shallow-Water Image Enhancement on an Edge NPU for Underwater IoT Imaging\n\n##### Guo, J.\n\n* * *\n\n#### 2026 5th International Conference on Artificial Intelligence, Internet of Things and Cloud Computing Technology, AIoTC 2026: 943-947\n\n#### 2026\n\n* * *\n\nISSN/ISBN: 9798319544346\n\nDOI: 10.1109/aiotc71250.2026.11689226\nAccession: 111552993\n\n##### Full-Text Article emailed within 0-6 h\n\n[Input][Input][Input][Input][Input][Input][Input] Buy Now for $19.90\n\nPayments are secure & encrypted\n\nImage: Powered by Stripe\n\nImage: Powered by PayPal\n\n##### Summary\n\nUnderwater image enhancement (UIE) research is trending toward heavy models, while lightweight alternatives are judged by parameters and FLOPs: proxies that say little about latency and energy on the edge accelerators underwater IoT platforms deploy. We report an on-device measurement study of shallow-water enhancement, whose per-channel attenuation physics favors compact quantized networks. Depthwise-separable U-Nets (9k-59k parameters) and a 4.3k-parameter curve model are trained on UIEB, quantized to FP16, INT8, and INT4 (palettized) with weight-only post-training quantization, and deployed via Core ML on the CPU, GPU, and Apple Neural Engine (ANE) of an Apple M3 Pro, with per-frame energy from idle-subtracted SoC power counters. The main 28.9k-parameter model at INT8 on the ANE enhances 256 × 256 frames at 945 FPS and 4.7 mJ/frame with no degradation at the reported two-decimal precision relative to FP16, and +3.2 dB PSNR over raw input on a 90-image held-out UIEB split. Across all 36 configurations on this FP16-compute stack, weight-only quantization provides no consistent latency or energy gain; energy is governed by compute-unit choice (3.6× vs. the GPU, 5.5-5.6× vs. the CPU) and network width. A W8A8 variant engages the ANE integer datapath (-18% latency, -28% energy at -0.37 dB), localizing the neutrality to weight-only PTQ rather than to the model or the NPU. A sub-30k-parameter network already saturates the measured quality-cost trade-off.\n\n* * *\n\nVinayak, B.S.V.; Yanamala, R.M.R.; David Amar Raj, R.D.A.; Pallakonda, A. 2026: Hierarchical mobile-dense convolutional architecture for tampered image detection using focal optimization with quantized edge TPU deployment Computers and Electrical Engineering 131: 110979\n\n* * *\n\nWan, X.; Wei, Q.; Xu, X.; Zhong, J.; Zhang, W.; Shen, L.; Ju, W.; Liang, Z. 2025: Underwater image enhancement via standard deviation reconstruction, dynamic sigmoid mapping and gradient-aware enhancement Optics and Lasers in Engineering 195: 109339\n\n* * *\n\nZhu, J. 2025: CENet: Color Selection and Edge Enhancement Network for Underwater Image Enhancement 2025 IEEE 5th International Conference on Electronic Technology, Communication and Information, ICETCI 2025: 170-175\n\n* * *\n\nPeng, C.; Wang, T. 2019: An improved energy-aware and self-adaptive deployment method for autonomous underwater vehicles International Journal of Modelling, Identification and Control 31(2): 182-192\n\n* * *\n\n【11†Li, P.\n* * *\n\nWang, X.; Zhang, E.; Wang, D.; Yan, X. 2026: Degradation-Aware Vision Transformer for Underwater Image Enhancement 2026 7th International Seminar on Artificial Intelligence, Networking and Information Technology, AINIT 2026: 949-958\n\n* * *\n\nJi, X.; Dai, C.; Hao, L.-Y.; Cai, C.; Liu, S. 2026: PAC-Net: Physics-Aware Constraint Network for Underwater Image Enhancement IEEE Sensors Journal 26(10): 14726-14735\n\n* * *\n\nWei, Z.; Zhang, J.; Fu, X.; Wang, H. 2026: Polarization-aware frequency modulation for und